{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's definitive test — 'removing the claim from the checker's input would change the checker's output; if not, it is restatement' — is incoherent under the covering principle that verification must produce an output not derivable from the claim's surface form. Any genuine execution or count produces a world-dependent result (e.g., a raw count, a program exit code) that is independent of the claim text, so removing the claim from the input cannot change that result; the test would classify every real execution as 'restatement.' Conversely, a paraphrase whose wording shifts because the claim is absent would pass SRC_001's test, yet is paradigmatic restatement. The counter-screen (requiring execution/count evidence elsewhere) partially patches this, but SRC_001 still elevates the broken counterfactual as 'the definitive test,' directly contradicting its own principle.","counterexample":["Claim: 'The list has 3 elements.' Checker executes len([a,b,c])=3. Removing the claim from the checker's input leaves the execution result unchanged at 3, so SRC_001's test labels this restatement, even though it is genuine verification by execution.","Claim: 'P implies P.' Checker with claim absent outputs 'P implies P is trivially true' vs. with claim present outputs 'The claim P implies P is trivially true' — output changes, so SRC_001's test labels this verification, even though it is mere restatement."],"target":"51f65c8e129297a9fdfff455256229e554ae30643116b43bf92d644ea5ad44f9"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's central concession — that same-engine execution counts as genuine verification even without engine separation — is undermined by its own analogy/disanalogy: it admits an LLM has a learned prior so self-checking 'may be contaminated,' yet never specifies what evidence within a single run's record would rule out such contamination for same-engine execution. The problem explicitly asks what record-internal evidence distinguishes genuine independent verification from mere restatement; for same-engine execution, SRC_002 only offers 'exact inputs, code, output observed,' but these are present even when the engine's prior steered the code toward a self-confirming bug, so the record cannot distinguish genuine verification from a systematic same-engine confound. Its countercondition acknowledges this gap yet leaves it as an unobserved 'if,' so the argument fails to meet the problem's evidentiary standard for the very case it defends.","counterexample":["A same-engine run records claim 'sum([1,2,3])=7' and checker code 'def f(x): return 6 if x==[1,2,3] else sum(x); print(f([1,2,3]))' producing 7. Inputs, code, and output are all recorded, exactly as SRC_002 requires, and the result could have contradicted — yet this is contaminated self-confirmation, not independent verification, and nothing in the single-run record as specified by SRC_002 distinguishes it from the genuine case."],"target":"de1003fa36f266e625214369e9da0d789b7d3fcb0ecbfcf440dd2e14978533a6"}]}